Bicipital Tendinitis
Musculoskeletal & Pain
Anterior shoulder pain, Tenderness in bicipital groove, Positive Speed's/Yergason's tests

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MKP-008
Reason for Visit: Severe, acute exacerbation of anterior shoulder pain preventing sleep and basic use of the dominant arm.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated a patient in extreme distress due to an acute flare of localized shoulder pain following heavy lifting, rendering them unable to move the arm or lie flat. Urgent clinical assessment was necessary to exclude an acute full-thickness rotator cuff tear or glenohumeral dislocation, and to administer potent acute analgesia to break the pain cycle prior to morning hours.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My shoulder is locked in agony; every time I try to lift my arm or lie down, a sharp pain shoots down my bicep."
History of Present Illness: Patient presents with severe acute-on-chronic right anterior shoulder pain. Timeline: Insidious mild throbbing ache over the past month; acutely deteriorated 12 hours ago after repeatedly lifting heavy boxes into a high cabinet. Currently reports a deep, throbbing ache localized to the anterior shoulder that radiates inferiorly along the anterior humerus. Severe exacerbation during any attempted lifting or overhead movement. Sensation of catching in the anterior shoulder. Night pain is profound, explicitly exacerbated by rolling onto the affected shoulder, preventing any sleep.
Functional Status: Immobilized right upper limb; patient guarding arm against chest.
Social/Safety Concerns: Severe sleep deprivation compounding acute pain crisis.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 146/88 | HR: 90 | Temp: 36.7 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 85 Kg
General: Alert, visibly fatigued, actively supporting the right forearm with the contralateral hand.
Physical Exam: Inspection reveals normal shoulder contours; no squaring or obvious muscular atrophy. Distinct, exquisite point tenderness on direct palpation of the long head of the biceps tendon within the intertubercular groove. Active range of motion is limited by pain in forward flexion. Passive range of motion is preserved but painful at the extremes. Positive Speed's test: severe pain in the bicipital groove during resisted forward elevation of the shoulder with an extended elbow and supinated forearm. Positive Yergason's test eliciting pain during resisted forearm supination. Rotator cuff testing (empty can, external rotation against resistance) reveals 5/5 strength with only secondary radiating pain.
Environmental Observations: Patient sleeping in a recliner chair due to inability to lie supine in bed.
Medication Reconciliation: Paracetamol 1g taken with no efficacy.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Bicipital Tendinitis - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Rotator Cuff Tear - Ruled out clinically by maintained 5/5 strength in the supraspinatus and infraspinatus testing, despite pain.

Glenohumeral Dislocation - Ruled out by normal anatomical contour of the shoulder and preserved, albeit painful, passive range of motion.

Cervical Radiculopathy (C5/C6) - Ruled out by negative Spurling's test, absence of distal paresthesia, and normal biceps/brachioradialis reflexes.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with an acute, sleep-preventing pain crisis localized to the anterior shoulder following repetitive mechanical overload. Clinical examination isolated the pathology to the long head of the biceps tendon, explicitly demonstrating positive Speed's and Yergason's tests with no evidence of gross motor structural failure of the rotator cuff. The severity of the pain resulted in a locked limb and total inability to sleep, creating an acute crisis. Delaying evaluation until daytime hours would have subjected the patient to unmanageable distress and sleep deprivation. Urgent after-hours assessment safely excluded surgical structural failures (tears/dislocations) and permitted the immediate initiation of targeted pharmacological and mechanical offloading strategies.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed oral Naproxen 500mg BD with food for 5-7 days and a short course of Codeine 30mg PRN for nocturnal pain control.
Positive Behavioral Change:
Action: Use a broad arm sling strictly for the next 48 hours to carry the weight of the forearm. Consequence: Immediate mechanical offloading of the biceps tendon, resulting in a rapid decrease in the constant, dull, resting ache (negative reinforcement).
Action: Sleep with a thick pillow wedged beneath the affected arm while lying on the back. Consequence: Prevention of the shoulder dropping backward into extension, avoiding the sudden, sharp pain spikes that cause waking (positive reinforcement).
Home-Based Services (Optional): Physiotherapy referral recommended for rotator cuff and scapular stabilization exercises.
DME/Safety (Optional): Provided a standard poly-sling.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 7 days for targeted ultrasound if symptoms fail to improve with NSAIDs and rest.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if there is a sudden appearance of a large, painful bulge in the middle of the upper arm (Popeye deformity), or if the shoulder joint becomes completely unmovable and locked.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical. Dynamic musculoskeletal ultrasound is required if conservative management fails. Conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: The acute inflammatory phase will settle over 3-7 days with NSAIDs and rest. Complete resolution requires modifying the lifting biomechanics that caused the initial friction in the tendon sheath.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek emergency care if your bicep muscle suddenly bunches up into a painful ball in the middle of your arm, or if you develop a fever and the shoulder becomes hot and red.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The biceps tendon runs through a tight bony groove in your shoulder. Overuse has caused it to swell and rub aggressively against the bone. The sling and anti-inflammatories work together to stop the friction and reduce the swelling inside that tight tunnel.
Actionable Steps: Wear the sling continuously for the next two days to take the weight of your arm off the inflamed tendon. Use the pillow wedge tonight so your shoulder doesn't hang backward and stretch the damaged tissue while you sleep.

